  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and `src/aura-assets.ts`. `typecheck` passes, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open the page in a browser, so the rendering and the fades are untested.\n\n- **Scene:** `/assets/soldier.glb` is loaded as a typed asset and scaled to 1.8 units tall with its feet at y=0. It stands on a 14×14 ground plane, and a perspective camera frames the full body at roughly 65% of the frame height.\n- **Light:** there is one shadow-casting directional light (2048 shadow map), plus a hemisphere fill light that casts no shadow. The soldier casts shadows and the ground receives them.\n- **Animation:** the soldier starts on `Idle`. Pressing `R` or `I` calls `app.nodes.require(\"soldier\").play(clip, { loop: true, crossFade: 0.4 })`. This relies on the engine's crossfade-on-clip-change path, which I read in the source but did not see run.\n- **Eval hooks:** `window.__EVAL__.clip()` returns the target clip name as soon as a fade starts. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set to `true` after `app.ready()` and two animation frames. It is also set if loading fails, with the error logged to the console.\n- **Hash placeholder:** the asset's `hash` in `src/aura-assets.ts` is a placeholder (`sha256-` followed by zeros), because I had no shell to compute the real one. The engine only checks that a hash is present and doesn't verify it against the file. The clip names (`Idle`, `Run`, `Walk`) come from the task text, not from inspecting the file.\n- **Bounds guess:** the declared bounds `[0.7, 1.8, 0.5]` are my estimate for a standard soldier model. If the real model's height isn't its largest dimension, the 1.8-unit scaling will be slightly off.\n\n",
  "finalTypecheckPass": true,
